// EXPECT_RUNTIME_PANIC assert failed: n == 999
//
// Registry 221.1 #679: the failed assert's message was formatted into a
// `char buf[512]` ON THE STACK, handed to the fail-frame as
// `nova_str_from_cstr(buf)` -- a NON-OWNING view -- and then `longjmp`'d off
// that very frame. Nothing copied it afterwards: the parent slot kept the raw
// pointer and it was read later, on the drive thread, with the child's stack
// long gone. A use-after-free across a thread boundary, and what the user saw
// was `panic:` with no text at all.
//
// The cause is gone: `nova_assert_loc` heap-copies once, at the source
// (0d4622dda). Nothing pinned that, though -- no fixture in the corpus
// asserted that an assert's own message survives a cleanup, which is exactly
// how the defect reached a release-blocking row in the first place.
//
// The trigger was never `defer` as such but how much stack runs between the
// formatting and the read, which is why it read as intermittent and lived so
// long. A message-carrying `defer` is the cheapest deterministic stand-in.
module standalone.p679_assert_message_survives_cleanup

fn boom(n int) Io -> () {
    defer { println("cleanup ran with n=${n} and enough text to disturb the frame") }
    assert(n == 999)
}

fn main() Io -> () => boom(1)
